<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<topic id="Authentication" revisionNumber="1">
  <developerConceptualDocument
    xmlns="http://ddue.schemas.microsoft.com/authoring/2003/5"
    xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink">

    <introduction>
      <para>
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Smtp.SmtpClient</codeEntityReference>,
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Pop3.Pop3Client</codeEntityReference> and
        <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient</codeEntityReference> all authenticate using
        <externalLink>
          <linkText>SASL</linkText>
          <linkUri>https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4422</linkUri>
          <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
        </externalLink>
        (Simple Authentication and Security Layer) mechanisms. This topic explains how MailKit chooses a
        mechanism and how to choose one yourself.
      </para>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="Credentials">
      <title>Authenticating with a user name and password</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          After connecting, the <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailService.AuthenticationMechanisms</codeEntityReference>
          property contains the list of SASL mechanisms advertised by the server. When you call
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.MailService.Authenticate</codeEntityReference>
          with a user name and password (or an <codeEntityReference>T:System.Net.ICredentials</codeEntityReference>),
          MailKit picks the strongest mechanism that is supported by both the client and the server:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\AuthenticationExamples.cs" region="AuthenticateWithCredentials"/>
        <para>
          MailKit chooses from the mechanisms advertised by the server in the following order of preference:
        </para>
        <table>
          <tableHeader>
            <row>
              <entry><para>Mechanism</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Notes</para></entry>
            </row>
          </tableHeader>
          <row>
            <entry><para>SCRAM-SHA-512(-PLUS), SCRAM-SHA-256(-PLUS), SCRAM-SHA-1(-PLUS)</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                The password is never sent to the server. The <literal>-PLUS</literal> variants are preferred
                when the server advertises them because they bind the authentication to the TLS session (see
                <link xlink:href="#ChannelBinding">Channel binding</link>).
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>NTLM</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                See <link xlink:href="Authentication-NTLM-Kerberos"/>.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>DIGEST-MD5, CRAM-MD5</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                Legacy challenge-response mechanisms based on MD5. Skipped on platforms where MD5 is
                unavailable (such as when FIPS mode is enforced).
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>PLAIN, LOGIN</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                The password is sent to the server (base64-encoded, not encrypted), so these should only be used
                over a TLS connection.
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </table>
        <para>
          If none of these mechanisms are available, <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Imap.ImapClient</codeEntityReference>
          falls back to the IMAP <literal>LOGIN</literal> command (unless the server advertises
          <literal>LOGINDISABLED</literal>) and <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Pop3.Pop3Client</codeEntityReference>
          falls back to the <literal>USER</literal>/<literal>PASS</literal> commands.
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Net.Pop3.Pop3Client</codeEntityReference> also uses the
          <literal>APOP</literal> command, in preference to SASL, if the server supports it.
        </para>
        <para>
          Mechanisms that require something other than a password, such as <literal>XOAUTH2</literal>,
          <literal>OAUTHBEARER</literal>, <literal>GSSAPI</literal> and <literal>ANONYMOUS</literal>, are never
          chosen automatically. To use them, see <link xlink:href="#SaslMechanisms">Choosing a mechanism</link>.
        </para>
        <para>
          If authentication fails, an <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.AuthenticationException</codeEntityReference>
          is thrown and the client remains connected so that you can try again.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="RemovingMechanisms">
      <title>Preventing MailKit from using a mechanism</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Some servers advertise mechanisms that do not work correctly (or that are not enabled for a particular
          account), and a few servers will drop the connection or lock the account after a failed attempt. To
          prevent MailKit from trying a mechanism, remove it from
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.IMailService.AuthenticationMechanisms</codeEntityReference> after
          connecting and before authenticating:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\AuthenticationExamples.cs" region="RemoveAuthenticationMechanisms"/>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="SaslMechanisms">
      <title>Choosing a mechanism</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          To use a specific mechanism, create an instance of the corresponding
          <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism</codeEntityReference> subclass and pass it to
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">M:MailKit.MailService.Authenticate(MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism,System.Threading.CancellationToken)</codeEntityReference>:
        </para>
        <code language="c#" source="Examples\AuthenticationExamples.cs" region="AuthenticateWithSaslMechanism"/>
        <para>
          The following mechanisms are available:
        </para>
        <table>
          <tableHeader>
            <row>
              <entry><para>Mechanism</para></entry>
              <entry><para>Class</para></entry>
            </row>
          </tableHeader>
          <row>
            <entry><para>ANONYMOUS</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismAnonymous</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>CRAM-MD5</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismCramMd5</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>DIGEST-MD5</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismDigestMd5</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>GSSAPI (Kerberos)</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismGssapi</codeEntityReference> (.NET 8 and later)</para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>LOGIN</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismLogin</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>NTLM</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlm</codeEntityReference> (managed) or
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismNtlmNative</codeEntityReference> (.NET 8 and later)
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>OAUTHBEARER</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismOAuthBearer</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>PLAIN</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismPlain</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>SCRAM-SHA-1(-PLUS)</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismScramSha1</codeEntityReference>,
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismScramSha1Plus</codeEntityReference>
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>SCRAM-SHA-256(-PLUS)</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismScramSha256</codeEntityReference>,
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismScramSha256Plus</codeEntityReference>
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>SCRAM-SHA-512(-PLUS)</para></entry>
            <entry>
              <para>
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismScramSha512</codeEntityReference>,
                <codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismScramSha512Plus</codeEntityReference>
              </para>
            </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry><para>XOAUTH2</para></entry>
            <entry><para><codeEntityReference>T:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanismOAuth2</codeEntityReference></para></entry>
          </row>
        </table>
        <para>
          <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism.Create</codeEntityReference>
          and <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism.TryCreate</codeEntityReference>
          can be used to create a mechanism by name, and
          <codeEntityReference>M:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism.IsSupported(System.String)</codeEntityReference>
          checks whether MailKit supports a mechanism on the current platform.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="ChannelBinding">
      <title>Channel binding</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Channel binding ties the authentication exchange to the underlying TLS session. Even if an attacker
          manages to intercept the TLS connection (for example, by using a fraudulent certificate), the server
          will reject the authentication because the TLS session the client sees is different from the one the
          server sees.
        </para>
        <para>
          The <literal>SCRAM-*-PLUS</literal> mechanisms always use channel binding, and the NTLM and GSSAPI
          mechanisms can optionally use it. After authenticating, the
          <codeEntityReference>P:MailKit.Security.SaslMechanism.NegotiatedChannelBinding</codeEntityReference>
          property indicates whether channel binding was used.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="OtherTopics">
      <title>OAuth 2.0, NTLM and Kerberos</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Many providers (including Gmail and Microsoft 365) no longer allow password authentication and
          require OAuth 2.0 instead; see <link xlink:href="Authentication-OAuth2"/>. For authenticating
          to Microsoft Exchange and other servers that use Windows authentication, see
          <link xlink:href="Authentication-NTLM-Kerberos"/>.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <relatedTopics>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication-OAuth2"/>
      <link xlink:href="Authentication-NTLM-Kerberos"/>
      <link xlink:href="Connecting-To-A-Server"/>
    </relatedTopics>
  </developerConceptualDocument>
</topic>
